<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackageId>Harbor.SessionContext</PackageId>
    <Description>Harbor session context — compaction, system prompt assembly, token tracking, workspace inspection and session forking (#1624).</Description>
    <PackageTags>harbor;ai;agent;session;compaction;prompt</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    #1624: the session-context cohort (Sessions/* — compaction, prompt
    assembly, token tracking, workspace inspection, session forking, result
    trimming) extracted verbatim from Harbor.Application into its own
    assembly, plan A part 2/4 of the Application decomp split. One-way edge:
    SessionContext -> Application only. Application holds the shared
    vocabulary this cohort reads and does NOT bind any moved type:

      - Harbor.Application.Resilience (IRetryPolicy / RetryOptions) — the
        retry seam CompactionService drives for the summary LLM call. The
        CONTRACT files deliberately stay in Harbor.Application: the loop's
        RetryPolicy / DefaultToolRetryDecider implementations live in
        Harbor.AgentRuntime and read the same contracts, so moving them here
        would have split one vocabulary across two assemblies.
      - Harbor.Application.Sessions (IWorkspaceContextSource /
        WorkspaceContextService) — the injectable workspace-context seam the
        static WorkspaceContextSource forwarder delegates to (#1523). The
        static forwarder moved (it is the workspace half of this cohort); the
        service and its interface stayed in Application, which is the
        leaf-over-Abstractions assembly the cohort reads them from.

    Two files moved BEYOND the issue's list, and both because the cohort
    bound them in code (a reference either way would have flipped the edge
    into a cycle):

      - Sessions/CompactionView.cs — the default ICompactionView
        implementation (#1612), a forwarder over the CompactionPolicy
        statics that moved with them.
      - Skills/SkillProvider.cs — the default ISkillProvider implementation,
        which reads the workspace context through WorkspaceContextSource.

    Namespaces are UNCHANGED (Harbor.Application.Sessions /
    Harbor.Application.Skills) per the NetArchLayerRules backward-compat
    convention, so every consumer keeps compiling: the move is rename-only.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Harbor.Abstractions\Harbor.Abstractions.csproj"/>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Harbor.Extensions\Harbor.Extensions.csproj"/>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Harbor.Application\Harbor.Application.csproj"/>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions"/>
    <PackageReference Include="ZLinq"/>
    <!--
      ZLinq.DropInGenerator emits source-generated extension methods that take
      priority over System.Linq's. Combined with `<Using Remove="System.Linq" />`
      below, every LINQ call site in Harbor.SessionContext resolves to ZLinq's
      zero-allocation pipeline without rewriting call sites (moved verbatim from
      Harbor.Application, which does exactly this — same setup as Abstractions,
      Registries and AgentRuntime).
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="ZLinq.DropInGenerator" ExcludeAssets="runtime">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <!-- Proj0037 (#1857): the package ships an analyzer and no runtime
           assembly, so the runtime asset is excluded explicitly. -->
    </PackageReference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Using Remove="System.Linq"/>
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
